exercise: {n} {t}, {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them. How many {t}?
exercise: {a} splits {n} {t} into {p} equal heaps and takes {m} heaps. How many {t} does {a.he} take?
exercise: In a classroom in Texas there are {n} {t}, and {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them are blue. How many blue {t} are there?
exercise: A shop in Dublin has {n} {t}, and it sells {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them. How many {t} does it sell?
exercise: {a} donates {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of {a.his} {n.words} {t} to a charity. How many {t} does {a.he} donate?
exercise: There are {n} {t}, and {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them are in a bag. How many {t} are in the bag?
test: {a} shares {n} {t} fairly among {p} children and then collects the shares of {m} of them. How many {t} does {a.he} collect?
test: Find {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of a pile of {n} {t}.
test: Of the {n} {t} at a school fete in Kent, {m.words} {p.ordwords}s are sold by noon. How many {t} are sold by noon?
test: In a box of {n} {t}, {m.words} out of every {p} are red. How many red {t} are in the box?
test: {a}'s aunt in Toronto keeps {n} {t} and gives {a} {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them. How many {t} does {a} get?
test: A family in Auckland owns {n.words} {t}. The children own {m.words} {p.ordwords}s of them, and the parents own the rest. How many {t} do the children own?
